From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add nyx-test-ledcontroller tool

nyx-modules.bb has packaged ${bindir}/nyx-test-ledcontroller in
FILES:${PN}-tests for years, but nothing has ever built it - neither upstream
nor any LuneOS patch. This makes the entry real.

It matters more now that the core configuration carries a colour. Nothing in
the running system sets one yet: CoreNaviLeds only ever passes brightness, and
the LunaNext.Common "Leds" QML singleton that wraps it has no callers at all.
Without a way to drive the device by hand there is no way to tell a module that
ignores the colour from hardware that cannot show one, which are very different
problems.

  nyx-test-ledcontroller -c red set
  nyx-test-ledcontroller -c green -b 64 set
  nyx-test-ledcontroller -c blue --on 500 --off-ms 2000 pulsate
  nyx-test-ledcontroller off
  nyx-test-ledcontroller -b 200 set        # no colour: the greyscale path
  nyx-test-ledcontroller -l backlight --lcd 50 set

Omitting -c sets no colour parameters at all, so the same binary exercises both
the colour path and the pre-colour behaviour it has to stay compatible with.

Built unconditionally rather than under if(NYXMOD_OW_LED). That flag selects
which of nyx-modules and nyx-modules-hybris provides the LED module; the tool is
a client of whichever won. On a hybris machine the flag is FALSE, and that is
precisely where the notification LED lives and where poking it by hand is most
useful.

NYX_ERROR_DEVICE_UNAVAILABLE is reported rather than swallowed, with a note
about NYXMOD_OW_LED: on a device with a real RGB LED that error means the
module is what is missing, not the hardware, and that is the single most likely
thing to be confused about here.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [LuneOS specific]
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
diff -urN nm.orig/src/CMakeLists.txt nm/src/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/src/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-30 21:13:35.007223923 +0200
+++ b/src/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-30 21:13:50.145559106 +0200
@@ -116,3 +116,10 @@
 if(NYXMOD_OW_HAPTICS)
     add_subdirectory(haptics_timedoutput)
 endif()
+
+# Not gated on NYXMOD_OW_LED. That flag says which of nyx-modules and
+# nyx-modules-hybris provides the LED module, and the tool is a client of
+# whichever won - on a hybris machine the flag is FALSE and the LED module is
+# built by the other recipe entirely, which is exactly where a way to poke the
+# notification LED is most wanted.
+add_subdirectory(led_controller_test)
diff -urN nm.orig/src/led_controller_test/CMakeLists.txt nm/src/led_controller_test/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/src/led_controller_test/CMakeLists.txt	1970-01-01 01:00:00.000000000 +0100
+++ b/src/led_controller_test/CMakeLists.txt	2026-08-30 21:13:50.124130498 +0200
@@ -0,0 +1,21 @@
